WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.390
We will study the development of the gastrointestinal tract and the peritoneal

2
00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.820
cavity and peritoneum in the developmental movement of the stomach,

3
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.330
duodenum, intestine, and colon.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.360
We will begin by understanding it in the development of the stomach in relation to

5
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.090
the liver, spleen, and pancreas.

6
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.960
Let's understand the initial phase, what we call the delimitation of the embryo.

7
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.032
We saw the integration.

8
00:00:28.071 --> 00:00:31.871
of this external coelom into an internal coelom.

9
00:00:31.871 --> 00:00:33.777
We arrive at this stage.

10
00:00:33.777 --> 00:00:37.927
And here, we see both in the sagittal view and in the transverse view.

11
00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:41.501
We clearly see the integration of my peritoneal cavity.

12
00:00:42.591 --> 00:00:44.841
Don't forget the synchronicity of development.

13
00:00:45.071 --> 00:00:47.791
One last image to see this integration.

14
00:00:49.251 --> 00:00:53.891
The main driver is growth, it's the formation of our cortex, but that's elsewhere.

15
00:00:56.142 --> 00:01:01.282
primitive, the overall growth of my embryo, the coiling and return to my midline.

16
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:07.032
When you take a small baby, you hold it in your hands and it shows you the whole

17
00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:08.472
history of its embryology.

18
00:01:08.472 --> 00:01:10.792
You just have to follow the tissues. It's

19
00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:15.632
a three-dimensional movement in four and even in the fifth dimension.

20
00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:17.922
I mean by fourth dimension, time.

21
00:01:17.922 --> 00:01:21.282
The fifth dimension, I mean that it is this energetic,

22
00:01:22.622 --> 00:01:26.862
electrical component. overall that underlies the entire system.

23
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:32.863
Don't forget that this movement is always integrated into this ascending phase of my

24
00:01:32.863 --> 00:01:38.333
ectoderm and relative descent of my endoderm, with this phase almost at the level of the heart.

25
00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:45.703
The ascent of the ectoderm and the descent of the endoderm and the integration of the heart

26
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:51.863
stretch the primordia of the diaphragmatic musculature and those of the future muscles.

27
00:01:51.863 --> 00:01:54.453
This means that to treat and reorganize this entire space,

28
00:01:54.453 --> 00:01:58.123
you will need to understand that you will have to treat and release the diaphragm,

29
00:01:58.123 --> 00:01:59.745
because it is the one that will create

30
00:01:59.745 --> 00:02:01.785
this global delimitation.

31
00:02:02.524 --> 00:02:07.104
The ascent of my neural tube, the development of my peritoneal cavity,

32
00:02:07.104 --> 00:02:09.004
the placement of my heart is

33
00:02:10.664 --> 00:02:15.144
a movement that will reintegrate globally towards a point of attachment.

34
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:17.854
which we call the umbilical cord.

35
00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:21.204
We could say that the umbilical cord, in its finality,

36
00:02:21.204 --> 00:02:25.044
contains the history of this development, of this unfolding, of

37
00:02:30.415 --> 00:02:33.773
this delimitation, of this reorganization of your peritoneum.

38
00:02:33.773 --> 00:02:37.193
This will integrate into specific rotational movements,

39
00:02:37.193 --> 00:02:40.673
organized in clockwise and counter-clockwise directions.

40
00:02:40.673 --> 00:02:42.733
On a frontal plane, we will see a

41
00:02:48.426 --> 00:02:51.506
global counter-clockwise developmental movement.

42
00:02:51.506 --> 00:02:55.906
On a transverse plane, we will see a clockwise developmental movement.

43
00:02:55.906 --> 00:02:58.826
This peritoneal cavity will begin to organize,

44
00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:02.526
to delimit itself.

45
00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:07.216
This means that it will move, and in this movement, you will have to learn to follow

46
00:03:07.216 --> 00:03:07.986
this development.

47
00:03:07.986 --> 00:03:11.426
First, there is the peritoneum that surrounds an organ. This

48
00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:13.756
is called a visceral peritoneum.

49
00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:15.266
When a peritoneum...

50
00:03:15.266 --> 00:03:19.446
connects an organ to the posterior part, it is called a mesentery.

51
00:03:19.446 --> 00:03:22.866
If it's the stomach, it will be called a mesogastrium.

52
00:03:24.857 --> 00:03:28.337
If it's the duodenum, it will be called the mesoduodenum.

53
00:03:28.337 --> 00:03:31.337
If it's a colon, it will be called the mesocolon.

54
00:03:31.337 --> 00:03:36.217
Or, depending on the location, it will be called a fascia of Tolte, for example.

55
00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:42.527
But if it's a small intestine, it's called a mesentery. Peritoneum that has the...

56
00:03:44.568 --> 00:03:49.628
Here, there is the posterior part, it is called the posterior parietal peritoneum.

57
00:03:50.988 --> 00:03:54.468
Laterally, it is called the lateral parietal peritoneum,

58
00:03:54.468 --> 00:03:56.828
the anterior parietal peritoneum.

59
00:03:59.668 --> 00:04:03.668
If it is a peritoneum that connects one organ to another organ,

60
00:04:03.668 --> 00:04:05.188
it is called an omentum.

61
00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:11.788
For example, you have the lesser omentum, which is a tissue that connects the liver

62
00:04:11.788 --> 00:04:12.535
to the stomach.

63
00:04:12.639 --> 00:04:15.618
You also have an omentum that connects the spleen to the stomach.

64
00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:21.419
An omentum, a mesentery, is always a double layer that carries a vessel right in the middle.

65
00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:28.119
But in the developmental movement, we will see that there will be folds and a

66
00:04:28.119 --> 00:04:32.259
reorganization that makes the omentum that is here, for example the mesentery,

67
00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:34.269
able to come to the side like this.

68
00:04:42.911 --> 00:04:47.591
And so here, there is a tissue that will attach and it will form an adhesion fascia.

69
00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:53.644
This is something that surgeons can resect without problem because there are no vessels.

70
00:04:53.771 --> 00:04:56.185
A mesentery always contains a vessel.

71
00:04:56.231 --> 00:05:00.591
Whereas a ligament or a fascia does not necessarily contain vessels.

72
00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:04.431
At the level of the peritoneum, we will have the mesoderm,

73
00:05:04.431 --> 00:05:07.621
the somatopleure, the splanchnopleure, the mesogastrium,

74
00:05:07.621 --> 00:05:08.911
ventral d'or...

75
00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:12.492
and we will have what are called mesenteries, fascias,

76
00:05:12.492 --> 00:05:15.472
omenta that we call epiploons or ligaments.

77
00:05:17.782 --> 00:05:21.932
All of that is the hyperperitoneum, we could say, initially organized in the

78
00:05:21.932 --> 00:05:23.526
peritoneal cavity, I mean.

79
00:05:24.102 --> 00:05:27.632
My intestine will start to grow, my liver will develop,

80
00:05:27.632 --> 00:05:31.382
my stomach wants to express itself, my lungs are growing.

81
00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:34.962
All of this needs to find its place.

